// Build-A-Player College Football, All-Time: defensive linemen (the DL half of the defensive All-Time pool;
// data/cfb-db-legends.js has DB, data/cfb-legends.js and data/cfb-te-legends.js the offense). Loaded like them,
// only when a build is All-Time. Written by the DL/DB legends build, 2026-11-25 (generator and notes kept with the build:
// scratchpad cfbdef/legends/tools: dl_rows.py, the hand rows; build.py, Size, OVR, order and the tier step).
//
// ── Who is here ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 86 legends: 50 edge, 36 interior (dt), from 47 schools, each in his school's 2026 conference
// (data/cfb-teams.js codes; Nebraska in the Big Ten, Pitt and Syracuse in the ACC, Houston and Arizona State in the Big 12):
//   SEC 12 edge + 14 dt · Big Ten 15 edge + 8 dt · Big 12 4 edge + 4 dt · ACC 14 edge + 7 dt · Pac-12 1 dt · Mountain West 1 edge + 1 dt · Sun Belt 1 edge · MAC 2 edge · Independents 1 edge + 1 dt
// Like the tight ends, not every school has one: the list is the position's college greats. Who qualifies:
//   the Outland, Lombardi, Nagurski, Bednarik and Hendricks winners who played the line, the unanimous and consensus
//   All-Americans, the sack and forced-fumble record holders (Terrell Suggs' 24 sacks in 2002, Derrick Thomas' 27 in
//   1988 before the NCAA kept the stat, Elvis Dumervil's 10 forced fumbles, Khalil Mack's 16 career), each power
//   program's icons, and a school's best where it has a name (Akron's Jason Taylor, Troy's DeMarcus Ware).
//   Mostly 1980 to now; older only where a young fan knows the name (Lee Roy Selmon, Oklahoma 1972-75).
//   A pass rusher his school called an outside linebacker counts as an edge (Will Anderson Jr., Derrick Thomas,
//   Von Miller, Khalil Mack), as in data/dl-legends.js. Alabama and Florida State have four, no school more.
// Edge or interior: his college position, with the NFL file's rule (data/dls.js): a listed end at 285 lb or more
//   plays inside (dt). So Jonathan Allen (286), J.J. Watt (290), Mario Williams (290), Leonard Williams (302) and
//   DeForest Buckner (291) are dt; Reggie White (264 on Tennessee's 1983 roster), Da'Quan Bowers (280) and Julius Peppers (283) stay edge.
//   Bruce Smith played end and tackle at Virginia Tech; he is an edge (most sources list him at end).
//
// ── The row (data/cfb-players.js's shape for a defender, minus the season lines) ────────────────────────────
// starter: true on every row; depth: his order at his slot type at the school, the greatest (highest ovr) first;
//   slot: EDGE1 for a school's first edge, EDGE2 for the rest, DT1 for an interior man; pos 'dl'; espnPos: his college
//   listing (DE, DT, NG, OLB). years: the seasons he played at the school. school: the school's name.
// height (in) / weight (lb): his college playing size, in this order of sources: ESPN's college record (about 2015
//   on); the school's listing in his peak year where one was found (Reggie White 6-5, 264 on Tennessee's 1983 roster;
//   Dorsey 6-2, 303 on LSU's 2007 one; Wistrom 6-5, 255; Freeney 255 at Syracuse; Hennings 6-6, 260, O'Neal 245, Bruce
//   Smith 6-4, 275 and Zorich 6-1, 268 from their College Football Hall of Fame pages); else his combine or pro-day
//   measurements (Wikipedia's pre-draft tables, 6'2 1/2" rounded to 75); else Wikipedia's listed weight (Casillas,
//   Waldrop). Corey Simon's 293 is an estimate between Florida State's 275 (1997) and his 297 pro listing.
//   number: his college number where known, left out where not sure.
// espn: the ESPN athlete id his record was matched on (his NFL page's where he has one, else his college page's);
//   left out where ESPN's search could not tell him from another man of the same name.
// line: his college resume in one line (awards, records, numbers), the evidence his grades were built from.
//
// ── Grades (0-11: F D C- C C+ B- B B+ A- A A+ S, the college scale of data/cfb-players.js and data/cfb-legends.js) ──
// Graded on what he did IN COLLEGE at his peak college season (the line: the honors and numbers used), never on his
// NFL career, and never copied from a rating anywhere: every trait was built from the evidence, 2026-11-25.
// Evidence, per man: his college numbers (ESPN's college stat pages for the men who played from about 2004 on, the
// schools' record books, NCAA leader lists, the schools' and the College Football Hall of Fame's bios), his awards
// and All-America teams (consensus and unanimous as the NCAA counts them), and his draft-class scouting (combine 40,
// 10-yard split, bench, height and weight; a college Speed follows his 40). Facts were checked against those sources
// and Wikipedia's facts; nothing was taken from a game's or a site's ratings.
// Trait by trait: Get Off from sacks, TFL and pressure per game and the 10-yard split; Bend from sack production
//   around the edge and his frame; Power and Finesse from how his sacks and TFL came (a bull rusher Power, a spin or
//   speed rusher Finesse); Strength and Block Shed from run defense, his weight and bench, double teams faced (a nose
//   gets them both: Sweat, Shelton, Jordan Davis); Play Recognition from his tackle totals, run stops and honors;
//   Speed from his 40 (see the Speed step below). The greats are A+/S on their signature traits; weak traits are honestly low (a 366-lb nose's Bend).
// S (11) only where it was generational: Aaron Donald's get-off (28.5 TFL from tackle), Suh's power and strength
//   (2009: 24 TFL, 12 sacks, a Heisman finalist), Derrick Thomas' get-off and bend (27 sacks), Suggs' bend (24 sacks),
//   Clowney's get-off, Freeney's first step.
// Size is never graded by hand: data/dls.js's dlSizeGrade on his height and weight against his spot (85% weight,
//   15% height; edge 233-280 lb, dt 257-345 lb), so a light rusher reads low (Hugh Green at 225, Corey Moore at 225).
// ovr: utils/simulation.js calcOVRDL put through utils/cfbOvr.js collegeOVR, the game's own numbers.
// One tier step after the hand grades (the first pass read a median of 92, above the offense legends' 87-89): about
//   20 men at the top of the position (the consensus all-time names and the multiple-award winners, build.py KEEP)
//   keep their grades as written; every other row loses one grade on each trait of A- or better outside his two
//   signature traits, so a one-award All-American reads 86-91 and the true greats pull away. Set by hand after it:
//   Hugh Green, Lee Roy Selmon and Derrick Thomas up to the top, where the all-time lists have them; Orakpo, Josh Allen, Dumervil, Fairley,
//   Bowers, Wistrom, Woodley, Rocker, Hennings, Corey Moore, Waldrop, Sweat and Jordan Davis kept at their
//   multiple-award level; Emtman up, Peppers, Garrett and Freeney down a step; Suh's block shed, Danny Shelton's
//   strength and Jordan Davis' block shed held at A+, not S; Derrick Thomas' recognition up to A+ (the Butkus Award).
// Speed step (last): where Wikipedia's pre-draft table has his 40, Speed is set from it (build.py SPEED40): an edge
//   4.43-4.48 A+, 4.52 A, 4.58-4.67 A-, 4.74-4.84 B+/B; a tackle 4.78 A-, 4.86-4.96 B+/B, 5.05-5.12 B-. So Freeney
//   (4.48) and Courtney Brown (4.52) went up, Suggs (4.84), Ware (4.65), Dorsey (5.12) and Ellis (5.05) down.
// Result, OVR p10 / median / p90 / max: edge 87/89/98/99 · dt 86/88/97/99
//   (99: Chase Young, Ndamukong Suh, Hugh Green, Aaron Donald, Bruce Smith).
// Rank order cross-checked against the names the all-time college lists agree on (ESPN's 150 greatest, the AP's
//   all-time All-America team, the College Football Hall of Fame; from those lists as known, not a fresh download):
//   Green, Selmon, Bruce Smith, Suh, Donald, Derrick Thomas and Reggie White are all in the top 17 here.
//
// ── Headshots (ESPN only) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 14 rows have one: headshot "cfb_{id}" = his ESPN college-football photo (public/headshots/cfb_{id}.webp, fetched
//   the way scripts/fetch-cfb-assets.mjs does it: ESPN's combiner, 350x254 crop, webp q75). Only men whose ESPN
//   college record has a photo (about 2015 on); every one was looked at on a contact sheet (posed college headshots, the
//   right man, the school's colors). No NFL, Wikipedia or Hall of Fame photos: everyone else shows initials.
//
// ── Checks (2026-11-25) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// On the output: every team is a data/cfb-teams.js code, no name twice, every trait 0-11, Size equals dlSizeGrade,
//   ovr equals the formula, and `npx esbuild` parses the file.
// Facts: ESPN's college stat pages confirmed the lines of the men from about 2015 on (Anderson 17.5 sacks 2021,
//   Josh Allen 17 and 5 FF, Chase Young 16.5 and 6 FF, Landry 16.5 and 6 FF, Garrett, Chubb, Hutchinson, Carter, ...).
//   Every award year in a line was checked against Wikipedia's winner tables for the Outland, Lombardi, Nagurski,
//   Bednarik and Hendricks (all match; Pollack won the Hendricks twice, 2003 and 2004). Searched and confirmed in
//   published sources: Donald's 2013 (28.5 TFL, 11 sacks), Hugh Green's 1980 (Heisman runner-up, three-time consensus
//   All-American, Pitt's 49 sacks), Bruce Smith (22 sacks in 1983, 46 career), Reggie White (15 in 1983, 32 career),
//   Simeon Rice (44.5, the Big Ten record), Bruschi (52), Leslie O'Neal (16 and 34, unanimous 1985), Hennings (24 sacks,
//   unanimous), Kearse (SEC Defensive Player of the Year 1998, 7.5 sacks that year), Ware's 2004 (Sun Belt Defensive
//   Player of the Year: 10.5 sacks, 19 TFL, 4 FF; Troy's career TFL leader) and Jason Taylor (two-time first-team
//   All-MAC, All-American as a junior in 1995). Every ESPN id was checked against ESPN's athlete record (name, college,
//   position); Reggie White's candidate ids were other men (an Oklahoma State back, a North Carolina A&T tackle), so his
//   row has none. Sports-reference's pages could not be read from the build (a bot check), so school record books were
//   reached through the schools' and the Hall of Fame's bios instead. Not confirmed: jersey numbers, Corey Simon's
//   1999 weight, and Casillas' and Waldrop's college weights (Wikipedia's listed ones).

